<Project>

  <!--
    THE version. Every other version value in the repo derives from this one, so a release is a
    one-character edit here instead of three edits that can silently disagree:

      src/Console.Lib/Console.Lib.csproj   VersionPrefix    $(ConsoleLibVersion).0
      src/Console.Lib/Console.Lib.csproj   AssemblyVersion  $(ConsoleLibVersion).0.0
      src/MdCat/MdCat.csproj               VersionPrefix    $(ConsoleLibVersion).0

    It is the X.Y only, because that is the part the three share; the patch/build component differs by
    consumer (the csprojs use .0 for local and debug builds, CI substitutes its run number).

    AssemblyVersion is in the list on purpose. It had drifted to 3.6.0.0 and stayed there through every
    bump from 4.1 to 4.8 while the other two moved together, which is exactly the failure a single source
    of truth removes. It was invisible because nothing compares them.

    mdcat's version is melded to the library's: CI passes one -p:Version to the whole solution, so the
    published tool tracks the library. Deriving its csproj value means the LOCAL build agrees too.

    STILL DUPLICATED, unavoidably: .github/workflows/dotnet.yml's VERSION_PREFIX, which must read
    X.Y.${{ github.run_number }}. A workflow's env: block cannot evaluate MSBuild, so the X.Y is repeated
    there. Making even that derived means turning it into a computed step (`dotnet msbuild
    -getProperty:ConsoleLibVersion` into $GITHUB_ENV) instead of a static env entry, which is worth doing
    but restructures the release-note block that currently lives in that env.

    Console.Lib.Inspector keeps its own VersionPrefix (1.0.0) and is deliberately NOT derived: it is a
    separately versioned MCP-server package. CI overrides it anyway via the solution-wide -p:Version.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ConsoleLibVersion>4.11</ConsoleLibVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
